{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Blue is the colour between violet and green on the spectrum of visible light.","title":"Blue"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Human eyes perceive blue when observing light with a wavelength between 450 and 495 nanometres. Blues with a higher frequency and thus a shorter wavelength appear more violet, while those with a lower frequency and a longer wavelength gradually appear more green.","title":"Blue"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Pure blue, in the middle, has a wavelength of 470 nanometres.","title":"Blue"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In painting and traditional colour theory, blue is one of the three primary colours of pigments, along with red and yellow, which can be mixed to form a wide gamut of colours.","title":"Blue"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Red and blue mixed together form violet, blue and yellow together form green.","title":"Blue"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Blue is also a primary colour in the RGB colour model, used to create all the colours on the screen of a television or computer monitor.","title":"Blue"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Three Colours: Blue (French: Trois couleurs : Bleu ) is a 1993 French drama film directed and co-written by Polish filmmaker Krzysztof Kieślowski.","title":"Three Colours: Blue"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" "Blue" is the first of three films that comprise the "Three Colours" trilogy, themed on the French Revolutionary ideals of liberty, equality, and fraternity; it is followed by "" and "".","title":"Three Colours: Blue"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" According to Kieślowski, the subject of the film is liberty, specifically emotional liberty, rather than its social or political meaning.","title":"Three Colours: Blue"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Hokusai Manga (北斎漫画 , "Hokusai's Sketches") is a collection of sketches of various subjects by the Japanese artist Hokusai.","title":"Hokusai Manga"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Subjects of the sketches include landscapes, flora and fauna, everyday life and the supernatural.","title":"Hokusai Manga"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The word "manga" in the title does not refer to the contemporary story-telling "manga", as the sketches in the work are not connected to each other.","title":"Hokusai Manga"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Block-printed in three colours (black, gray and pale flesh), the Manga comprise literally thousands of images in 15 volumes, the first published in 1814, when the artist was 55.","title":"Hokusai Manga"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The final three volumes were published posthumously, two of them assembled by their publisher from previously unpublished material.","title":"Hokusai Manga"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The final volume was made up of previously published works, some not even by Hokusai, and is not considered authentic by art historians.","title":"Hokusai Manga"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The "Three Colours" trilogy (Polish: "Trzy kolory" , French: "Trois couleurs" ) is a three-part film series directed by Krzysztof Kieślowski.","title":"Three Colours trilogy"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Two of the films were made in French and one primarily in Polish: "" (1993), "" (1994), and "" (1994).","title":"Three Colours trilogy"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" All three were co-written by Kieślowski and Krzysztof Piesiewicz (with story consultants Agnieszka Holland and Sławomir Idziak) and have musical scores by Zbigniew Preisner.","title":"Three Colours trilogy"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The City of Sydney flag is made up of a horizontal triband of three colours – white, gold and blue.","title":"City of Sydney flag"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was designed in 1908.","title":"City of Sydney flag"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The top third of the flag features three designs.","title":"City of Sydney flag"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The flag is displayed in Town Hall, Sydney.","title":"City of Sydney flag"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Three Colours: White (French: Trois couleurs : Blanc ) is a 1994 French-Polish comedy-drama film co-written, produced, and directed by Krzysztof Kieślowski.","title":"Three Colours: White"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" "White" is the second in "The Three Colors Trilogy", themed on the French Revolutionary ideals, following "" and preceding "".","title":"Three Colours: White"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film was selected as the Polish entry for the Best Foreign Language Film at the 67th Academy Awards, but was not accepted as a nominee.","title":"Three Colours: White"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: first
